l is inside `vim.schedule`, which runs AFTER `_do_incremental_streaming_render` returns. By that time, `message_start_line` should already be set (even for first chunk, it's set at line 3838). However, `vim.schedule` captures the closure at the time of creation, and `state.streaming.message_start_line` is accessed inside the closure, so if it's nil at creation time but set later before the callback runs, the callback will see the updated value. This seems OK.\n\nBut the real issue is: `apply_highlights(buf, start_line)` highlights from `start_line` to END_OF_BUFFER. For a streaming message that grows from 1 to 200 lines, each chunk's schedule callback processes from `message_start_line` (say line 50) to the current end (growing from 51 to 250). So the last few chunks process ~200 lines each.\n\nActually, this might not be as bad as I thought since `vim.schedule` coalesces calls in the same event loop iteration. If 10 chunks arrive in one event loop, only the last `schedule` callback runs. But streaming chunks typically arrive one at a time across separ...[truncated]
